  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Ohio has received $6.2B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The federal government awarded 51,267 contracts in Ohio so far, with an average award size of $121K. Obligations are down 36.9% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in the state, obligating $4.1B. GENERAL ELECTRIC COMPANY is the largest vendor, receiving $599M. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) is the largest industry by dollar value at $1.1B, followed by Petroleum Refineries at $580M and Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing at $534M. Other notable buyers include the General Services Administration at $458M and the Department of Energy at $437M. Other notable vendors include MARATHON PETROLEUM COMPANY LP at $526M and SOUTHERN OHIO CLEANUP COMPANY LLC at $322M. These figures are fiscal year to date totals and represent contract obligations, not completed spending.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the top buyers and how much did they obligate?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $4.1B. The General Services Administration obligated $458M, the Department of Energy obligated $437M, and the Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $347M. The Department of Homeland Security obligated $165M and the National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $161M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors captured the largest shares of obligations?",
        "body": "Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences captured $1.1B. Petroleum Refineries captured $580M and Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing captured $534M. GENERAL ELECTRIC COMPANY received $599M and MARATHON PETROLEUM COMPANY LP received $526M, with SOUTHERN OHIO CLEANUP COMPANY LLC receiving $322M."
      }
    ]
  },
